{"role":"assistant","content":"Done. The job was never failing to run — it failed to *speak*. Two stacked bugs, both visible in the snapshot:\n\n**Why it was silent**\n\n1. **The agent call failed every night.** `launchctl-print.txt` shows `runs = 6, last exit code = 1`, and `Library/Logs/nightly-review.err` has two lines per run: `Not logged in · Please run /login`, then `nightly-review.zsh:19: read-only variable: status`. Cause: the plist's `EnvironmentVariables` sets `PATH` but not `USER`, so the headless CLI can't find its keychain login when launchd spawns it (interactively it inherits `USER` from your shell, which is why it works by hand).\n2. **The failure reporter died before speaking.** `status=$?` at `bin/nightly-review.zsh:19` assigns to zsh's read-only `status` variable, a fatal error that kills the script on the spot — before the `if` that sends the FAILED Telegram message. I reproduced this exactly: exit 1, zero notifications.\n\n**Fixes**\n\n- `bin/nightly-review.zsh` — renamed `status` → `rc`; the prompt is now piped to the agent via stdin instead of passed as a multi-line argument; each run logs `exit=<code> duration=<seconds>` to `nightly-review.out`.\n- `Library/LaunchAgents/com.vk.nightly-review.plist` — added `USER=vk` to `EnvironmentVariables` (plist lints OK).\n\n**Verification** (stubbed `claude` + stubbed notifier, no external calls, since this is a snapshot): buggy script + failing agent → silent, exit 1 (your symptom reproduced); fixed script + failing agent → FAILED notification sent; fixed script + working agent → exit 0, summary notification, fresh review file, duration logged.\n\nFull details, including which parts of your colleague's note applied (the `launchctl print` check, the `status` trap, `USER` in the plist, stdin prompt, duration logging — all applied; `< /dev/null` and the watchdog caveat didn't), are in **ANSWER.md**, along with the steps to reload and `kickstart` the job on the real machine."}
